Give apps a Sailfish app cover (needs 0007, 0009).

Apps send pictures of their cover on the binary channel
flutter-sfos/cover_frame: width and height as little-endian uint32, then
RGBA with premultiplied alpha. The first picture creates a cover window: a
second wl_shell surface, transient for the main one, with the Qt surface
extension properties Silica apps use (cover: CATEGORY "cover", WINID 2;
main window: WINID 1, SAILFISH_COVER_WINDOW "__winref:2",
SAILFISH_HAVE_COVER). Lipstick then shows it in the app's tile instead of
the main window, and swaps the two itself when the app is opened, as for
native apps. Pictures go into wl_shm buffers. Lipstick's configure sets the
size, which depends on how many apps are open (446x714 or 284x454 on the
Jolla Phone 2026).

The JSON channel flutter-sfos/cover tells the app whether it can have a
cover, whether Lipstick shows it and its size, so it only draws the cover
when needed:

  {"version": 1, "available": true, "visible": false,
   "width": 446, "height": 714}

Apps can also give the cover buttons:

  {"version": 1, "actions": [{"id": 0, "icon": "icon-cover-refresh"}]}

The home screen draws them from the main window's _APP_COVER_ACTION
property and, when one is tapped, calls trigger(id) on a D-Bus name the
runtime owns (org.sailfishos.coveraction.<app>.pid<pid>.id1). The call
names no interface, so it's caught with a GDBus filter. The tap reaches the
app as {"version": 1, "action": id}.

Apps without a cover window are still shown as their main window. That
window now keeps its orientation while it's the cover, instead of turning
portrait (0007), which Lipstick animated in the tile.

The Dart side is the flutter_sailfish package (see docs/PORTING.md).

